  ],
  "text": "A computer beat the world chess champion in nineteen ninety seven. A robot walking reliably over rubble took another twenty years. Chess has a fixed board, a full view of it, and a turn in which to think. Walking has none of those three things. Standing still is not stable. You are a tall thing on two small feet, permanently falling. Your body corrects that hundreds of times a second, using signals you never notice. Ankle, hip, inner ear, the sole of your foot. All feeding a correction you did not decide on. And walking is worse. Walking is falling forward and getting a leg out in time. Every step is a controlled fall you recover from before it finishes. So the robot has to sense the tilt, predict the fall, and act before it arrives. Continuously. You find this easy only because you never had to know you were doing it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
